Eyewear innovations are coming into focus

While other tech companies like Meta and Google are doubling down on effort=
s to spin out smart glasses (https://gizmodo.com/pivoting-to-smart-glasses-=
is-getting-out-of-hand-2000711938?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9NX1WneNw8pxsZKEMEW_aXQY=
DF6sLRyh8c31J2HNLI2vozFsKLR-lOl4Zms8zhLnG5v8gKWW8liH9V0HsQ3Op77IBHDw&_hsmi=
=3D400542856 ) with futuristic specs, one eyewear startup is working on imp=
roving the world=E2=80=99s oldest wearable=E2=80=99s most basic function: v=
ision correction.

Finland-based IXI (https://ixieyewear.com/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9NX1WneNw8pxsZK=
EMEW_aXQYDF6sLRyh8c31J2HNLI2vozFsKLR-lOl4Zms8zhLnG5v8gKWW8liH9V0HsQ3Op77IBH=
Dw&_hsmi=3D400542856 ) is developing autofocus glasses that adjust to a use=
r=E2=80=99s vision on the fly, and it=E2=80=99s raised $40m in funding to b=
ring it to market. If it succeeds, it would be the eyewear industry=E2=80=
=99s first major innovation on vision correction in ~60 years.

Similar to bifocals and varifocals, IXI=E2=80=99s glasses would still have =
separate regions for different magnification, but it would provide a much m=
ore natural viewing experience with less distortion and a larger near-visio=
n area, per (https://www.cnn.com/science/autofocus-glasses-ixi-change-lense=
s-spc?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9NX1WneNw8pxsZKEMEW_aXQYDF6sLRyh8c31J2HNLI2vozFsKLR-=
lOl4Zms8zhLnG5v8gKWW8liH9V0HsQ3Op77IBHDw&_hsmi=3D400542856 ) CNN.

How it works

- Embedded sensors track a wearer=E2=80=99s eyes while infrared light is us=
ed to measure the reflection and predict what they=E2=80=99re looking at.

- That data is converted to electrical signals that inform liquid crystals =
within the lenses to adapt to the wearer=E2=80=99s needs.

What that could mean for people with trouble seeing far and near: no need t=
o own multiple pairs of glasses or peer through the bottom half of your len=
ses to read.

Other companies are also working on autofocus lenses

Japanese company Elcyo (https://en.elcyo.com/products?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9NX1=
WneNw8pxsZKEMEW_aXQYDF6sLRyh8c31J2HNLI2vozFsKLR-lOl4Zms8zhLnG5v8gKWW8liH9V0=
HsQ3Op77IBHDw&_hsmi=3D400542856 ) =E2=80=99s tech also uses liquid crystal =
lenses, while ViXion (https://vixion.jp/vixion01s/?wovn=3Den&_hsenc=3Dp2ANq=
tz-9NX1WneNw8pxsZKEMEW_aXQYDF6sLRyh8c31J2HNLI2vozFsKLR-lOl4Zms8zhLnG5v8gKWW=
8liH9V0HsQ3Op77IBHDw&_hsmi=3D400542856 ) (also Japanese) already offers aut=
ofocus glasses, though they have a narrower field of vision and look a bit=
=E2=80=A6 cyborg-y.

Which is what makes IXI=E2=80=99s product more consumer friendly: They=E2=
=80=99re lightweight and look like normal glasses =E2=80=94 something other=
 smart glass makers have struggled with =E2=80=94 despite all the hardware =
embedded within them.

The drawbacks?

One is cost, which CEO =E2=80=8B=E2=80=8BNiko Eiden told CNN would put the =
glasses =E2=80=9Cin the really high end of existing eyewear,=E2=80=9D addin=
g only that they=E2=80=99d be more expensive than the current options, whic=
h can retail for up to $1k+.

They=E2=80=99d also need to be charged overnight, which means one more devi=
ce to manage, with complicated repairs and some potentially dangerous impli=
cations. We can imagine a few scenarios that could be bad for, like when dr=
iving.

They=E2=80=99ll require further testing =E2=80=94 for conditions like tempe=
rature change, moisture, lighting, etc. =E2=80=94 before they=E2=80=99re sa=
fe to use on the road, though it does have a failsafe mode that would resto=
re the glasses to their base prescription in the event of a malfunction.

So, IXI has a few big kinks to work out before the high-tech spectacles, wh=
ich are due to launch within the next year, are ready for everyday use.

In any case, I guess you could say the future is looking clear AF.

